PDF: https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~kamalika/pubs/bcdkmt07.pdf  
**Exact location:** PDF p. 5 (viewer page index 4), Theorem 5 and Eq. (11), followed immediately by the statement that the relevant Fourier coefficients are “necessary and sufficient data” for the marginal.

**S2.** E. Ghorbani, S. Kamali, G. B. Khosrovshahi, D. S. Krotov, *On the volumes and affine types of trades*, arXiv:1810.02296 (2018).  
https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.02296  
**Exact location:** Introduction and §2.1; Lemma 3 states that a nonempty \([t]\)-unitrade has size at least \(2^{t+1}\), equivalently a \([t]\)-trade has volume at least \(2^t\). Definition Eq. (2) is the equal-low-order-incidence condition.

**S3.** I. Diakonikolas, D. M. Kane, *Degree-d Chow Parameters Robustly Determine Degree-d PTFs (and Algorithmic Applications)*, arXiv:1811.03491 (2018).  
https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.03491  
**Exact location:** abstract/introduction: degree-\(d\) Chow parameters are Fourier coefficients of degree at most \(d\), and their exact uniqueness for degree-\(d\) PTFs is stated as known background; the paper proves robust identifiability.

**S4.** J. Bruck, *Harmonic Analysis of Polynomial Threshold Functions*, SIAM J. Discrete Math. 3(2):168–177 (1990), DOI 10.1137/0403015.  
https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/0403015  
**Exact location:** abstract states a necessary-and-sufficient harmonic condition for an \(S\)-threshold function. This is an early antecedent in the polynomial-threshold / harmonic characterization line. The present audit did not inspect a full proof mapping every normalization used here to Bruck's notation.

**S5.** D. DeSantis, E. Skau, D. P. Truong, B. Alexandrov, *Factorization of Binary Matrices: Rank Relations, Uniqueness and Model Selection of Boolean Decomposition*, ACM TKDD 16(6), Article 112 (2022), DOI 10.1145/3522594; arXiv:2012.10496.  
https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.10496  
**Exact role:** distinguishes standard-arithmetic real/nonnegative/binary ranks from logical Boolean and \(\mathbb Z_2\) ranks and studies their relations. It is used here to keep rank notions separate, not as a source for the Q13/Q14 threshold itself.

**S6.** P. Lindner, S. B. John, C. Koch, D. Suciu, *The Moments Method for Approximate Data Cube Queries*, Proc. ACM Manag. Data 2(2) (2024), DOI 10.1145/3651147.  
Conference page: https://2024.sigmod.org/toc-2-2.html  
**Exact role:** partial materialization of data-cube aggregates via moment/Fourier-type expansions; approximate rather than the exact factor-profile destructive-update theorem proved in T3.

**S7.** General data-cube partial-materialization / thin-shell literature. A representative reviewed description is H. A. et al., *Partial materialization for online analytical processing over multi-tagged document collections*, Knowledge and Information Systems (2015), DOI 10.1007/s10115-015-0871-2.  
**Exact role:** establishes that materializing low-dimensional cuboids to trade storage for query time is standard background. This prevents claiming that “store low-order coordinate aggregates” is new in itself.

**S8. Companion CM-LM paper.** *Correspondence and Logical Matrices: A Typed Boolean Operator Calculus* (current project manuscript).  
**Exact location in current source:** theorem “Rank and conjunctive/XOR separability,” around source lines 1041–1067, proves that fixed-cut GF(2) rank equals the minimum number of conjunctive factors in an XOR decomposition and explicitly distinguishes GF(2) rank from OR-based Boolean rank. The new conditioned-query paper should cite this companion for that ingredient rather than reclaim it.

